<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Mauritius</h1><p class="st-meta">1638 CE – 1710 CE · capital Fort Frederik Hendrik</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dutch East India Company's settlement on Mauritius, which introduced sugar cane and saw the extinction of the dodo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sailors named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> after Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> in 1598, and in 1638 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>) founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-frederik-hendrik">Fort Frederik Hendrik</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-port">Grand Port</a> to forestall French and English rivals. The first commander was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-gooyer">Cornelis Gooyer</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The small settlement exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, cut from the island's forests, and served as a supply base for ships bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>. Enslaved people were brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and the East Indies, and the colonists introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, deer and pigs. Hunting and introduced animals contributed to the extinction of the dodo, last reliably seen in 1662.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The colony was abandoned in 1658, reoccupied in 1664 and finally abandoned in 1710 after cyclones, droughts and losses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> claimed the empty island in 1715 as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isle-de-france">Isle de France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-mauritius.jpg" alt="Dutch Mauritius" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_East_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1638 CE – 1710 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort Frederik Hendrik</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cornelis Gooyer</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritius">Mauritius</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mauritius (VOC)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1674&amp;focus=dutch-mauritius" aria-label="Dutch Mauritius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6230 2925 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dutch Mauritius lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6550 3181L5097 634M6550 3181L5097 634"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5097 634h0M5097 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6550 3181h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6550 3181h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1674&amp;focus=dutch-mauritius">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

